and that's a sharpie um what is my  favorite animal gag from the whole six  seasons of the show  it's it's impossible to pick one one  that comes to mind which is actually  written in the script that I think about  a lot because I'm very proud of it was  there's an episode where a ferret is  talking to Henry Winkler at a funeral  and being very inappropriate for the  funeral and Henry Winkler kind of shuts  her down and says I'll let you get back  to your business and gestures to a group  of other ferrets and a group of ferrets  is called a business of ferrets that is  a joke that delights me because I think  it's thing you might not notice on first  watch if you don't happen to that's that  piece of trivia yeah mostly I just sit  around the house and complain about  things yeah yeah how's that working out  for you  man I can't complain so it first started  because I was friends with Lisa Hanawalt  who is a brilliant artist we went to  high school together and we would joke  about you know one day we're gonna make  a cartoon together and we had various  terrible ideas but I always have wanted  to work with her on stuff and then she  went to UCLA and I went to New York for  college but we stayed in touch and then  we kind of switched places she moved to  New York and then I moved down to LA and  I started pitching shows you do a lot of  meetings here in Hollywood and so I got  you got any ideas  yeah I got some ideas we'd love to hear  them here they are okay well let us know  if you have any ideas I just told you  all my ideas what happened so I came up  with like three to five ideas and I said  eyes think about what direction my life  would take if we'd gone with one of the  other ideas instead of Bojack horseman  so I wanted to do a family comedy set  during the reign of terror in France  where the dad was a guillotine salesman  and business was booming but he couldn't  get his wacky family in order so that  was one idea I had I was gonna kind of  like all in the family but like in  France and his kids are gonna be  revolutionaries and like the ghost of  Marie Antoinette and be living in their  attic I think was gonna be called the  good times are killing me I think was  the idea because his the guillotines are  you know selling like hotcakes and  eventually I ran out of ideas what else  what else can I do what else can I come  up with and so I went to Lisa's blog and  I pulled a bunch of her drawings that  she had made because she'd been drawing  these animal people on her own and I  thought okay what can I do with these  but at the same time I had just moved to  LA and I was living in this house up in  the Hollywood Hills that was like a  friend of a friend of a friend's house I  somehow was paying like $800 a month to  live in this glorified closet in a  beautiful mansion and I don't quite know  how it was arranged but I remember the  rumors amongst the housemates  that was the third highest elevated  house in all of Hollywood that Johnny  Depp had lived there once there are all  sorts of secrets in the houses it was on  the on the top of this Hill up this very  windy road that I immediately got into a  car accident on and then I didn't have a  car and I was stranded up on this house  and I remember looking out on the deck  over all of Hollywood and feeling  simultaneously on top of the world and  never more isolated and alone I didn't  have any friends I didn't know anybody  and so that was the beginning of the  character for me the idea of this this  guy who was living in a house like this  who'd had every opportunity for success  but still couldn't find a way to be  happy so it's kind of that married with  Lisa's style is where the show came  together Joe was never the same after I  left admit it some people prefer the  later years the character Bojack was not  written with will in mind I think he  might be delighted to know you know  who'd be a great  washed-up sad lonely actor Will Arnett  no you know it was written just as for  Beau jag was written for this drawing  that Lisa Hanawalt had drawn of this  horse guy and then once we were  developing the project we thought you  know before we pitch this around it  might help to have some talent attached  so Linda LaMontagne is our casting  director and she kind of drew up a list  of you know here are some actors I think  would be right for this and as soon as I  saw Will Arnett's name I was like Oh  yes that's the guy because he's so funny  but there's also a darkness to him and I  feel like in his you know gravelly  performance you feel like he's lived a  life and there's like a sadness lurking  underneath there but again he's so funny  I mean there are lines that we have made  Bojack say that I would not have the  hutzpah to give to an actor because  they're so dopey but that I knew that  Will Arnett could pull them off like I  remember in the writers room like  looking at a line scripting like click  this line and this is so stupid  and we do the table read and Will Arnett  reads it and it's the funniest thing  you've ever heard oh my god you took our  dumbest stuff and he spun it into gold  and that's what you want on a chair you  want someone who elevates the material  and I am so blessed with our cast that  just every single one of them elevates  the material every single one of them is  better than the jokes you give them like  you can give them setups to jokes and  they make the setups funny you can give  them sad lines and they make them tragic  you can make them give them sad lines  they make them tragic and funny at the  same time somehow one of the funniest  things I've ever heard was Will Arnett  going what what the line was what and he  kills it yeah you know that is mr.  peanut butter Paul is also hilarious I  mean Paul is an incredibly talented  actor his instincts are dead-on and also  I think he preps I think he looks at the  script beforehand and makes choices and  thinks how am I gonna do this line what  is the funniest way to do this so he  comes in and he's just overflowing with  funny ideas you know when I was first  writing the character of mr. peanut  butter I initially heard him in my head  as like more of like a meathead more of  a deep kind of gravelly like a patrick  warburton type you know you can kind of  imagine like the big lunkhead but  because we cast will who has this deep  voice and then we cast Aaron as Todd and  Aaron also has a pretty deep voice I  felt like I if we have a third deep  voice guy it's just gonna get repetitive  and boring and so I wanted someone with  who had like a higher register and I  thought Paul had this beautiful lilting  quality to him it almost feels like he's  skipping across the line which I think  as a foil to Bojack is very funny that  Bojack he just even  his voice is very kind of sunk down and  in the muck whereas mr. peanut butter is  is playfully skipping above the muck I'm  responsible for my own happiness I can't  even be responsible for my own breakfast  I pitched it as a show that is gonna  start very fun and goofy like most adult  animated shows but by the end of its  first season it's going to turn into  more of a drama D along lines of a  live-action show like a girls or even  Mad Men I think we're the examples I  used at the time which felt a little  more unprecedented in the world of  animation to indulge that much in  seriousness and emotion but that was the  ideas that we were gonna trick people by  making people think it was a typical  animated sitcom but actually by the end  of the season they'd be going oh my god  why do I have feelings where did this  come from I think in my plan I always  assumed the audience will join us on  this ride and go from oh this is fun  this is silly to oh my god I have  feelings it never occurred to me that  some people might see the silly part and  go not for me click and not even know  that the rest of the show turns into  this totally other thing I think if I  were doing it again I might try to  include more clues at the beginning that  this was a more serious show but even  then I think it's hard to do because  there are clues in the beginning and I  think because of the context of it being  an adult animated show people didn't see  those clothes because they assumed they  knew what it was right and so they saw  the clues that affirmed what they  thought it was but they missed the stuff  that didn't point in that direction in  the first episode of the show Bojack and  Diane have like a two-minute  conversation on the fleeting nature of  happiness right so that should be a hint  that like oh that's what the show is  really interested in but I remember  reading reviews of the first season and  one said you know for a show that is  meant to be just wall-to-wall jokes  there sure are a lot of scenes that  don't have that many jokes and I thought  you're so close your first instinct is  what is this like that I've seen before  and I think what I tried to do was make  it kind of like what people had seen  before but also have one foot in  something kind of new  and challenging and I think if I were  doing it again  I wouldn't spend as much time in the  what audiences have seen before I don't  think they needed their hands held as  much as I thought they did I think  audiences were ready for something new  and something different because that's  the thing that I really loved about the  first season is that it did that change  and that it was both of those things  that it wasn't just one or the other and  so when we were working on season two I  definitely felt the confidence of like  let's go further in that direction  because that's what the show is that's  that's the sweet spot that's that's what  people are interested in is this  emotional stuff wall at the same time  it's a goofy silly fun cartoon where did  the theme song come from that's a great  question so Andy Gowen is our music  supervisor and he kind of put the word  out to a bunch of different bands and a  bunch of different artists hey you know  we're here's a new show we're looking  for some some music and we had a lot of  responses and and the to that kind of  rose to the top for me was this very  playful sing songy group love song and  this very heavy spooky Patrick Carney  and Ralph Carney song and I remember  there was a lot of debate over which  song are we gonna use and then we put  together this main title sequence the  director Mike Roberts but there's an  incredible sequence of Bojack kind of  going through his day and we played  different songs over to see what felt  right and we'd bring in people from the  crew we did focus groups and was like  pretty evenly split like people love the  group love song and people loved the  Patrick Carney Ralph Carney song and at  the end of the day we thought could we  do both and so we ended up using the the  Patrick Carney Ralph Carney song at the  beginning and the group of song at the  end I think they do different things in  really fun ways you know I really like  that the the Carney song has more of a  spooky melancholy feel to it I think it  is a little bit of a hint of what's to  come in the show and it does make you  think like oh this feels a little more  dramatic than like a typical animated  fun sitcom and then I think the group  love song kind of the opposite is so fun  and bright and at the end of some of our  efforts  which are really downer endings like it  almost feels like an ironic like you  know poking the ribs like it's it's  almost taunting and it almost brings out  the emotionality in a fun new way  there's also darkly humorous and so I've  really enjoyed using that song as  juxtaposition against all these  different endings especially as the show  has gotten more and more serious it's  it's kind of revealed itself to have new  layers to it  [Music]
 

